{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791372262,"nonce":"eYZmIWtpY9HwDUdRC63ryD8Zhuk=","title":"Conditional abstract methods lose receiver mutation through nested method calls","message":"Native Haxe --interp accepts the following and prints CONFORMANCE_OK:\n\n    enum Flag { A; B; }\n    var flags = new haxe.EnumFlags\u003cFlag\u003e();\n    flags.setTo(B, true);\n    if (flags.toInt() != 2) throw \"setTo writeback\";\n    flags.setTo(B, false);\n    if (flags.toInt() != 0) throw \"setTo unset\";\n\nThe pinned Cranelift probe /tmp/rayzor-abstract-mutation-native/EnumFlagsConditional.hx fails with \"setTo writeback\" in /tmp/rayzor-enumflags-conditional-v5.log. The imported body of EnumFlags.setTo branches between set(v) and unset(v). Expanding the conditional still calls those methods by value, so their writes to this cannot reach the caller. Nested abstract calls and bodies with control flow need receiver-aware expansion; the current straight-line body expansion deliberately does not cover this scope.\n\nRelated parent: 42d28bbb785f5c90c8e08dc3ccc15a5df6b773174e9633c5a3dde1ba88ae1b37. Its direct EnumFlags.set/unset reproducer now passes with imported straight-line expansion, but keep this remaining nested-call scope open.","files":null}]}